# 11. Dockerfile vs Docker Compose vs Backend Responsibility

This is VERY important.

---

# Dockerfile Responsibility

Dockerfile is used for:

* building image
* installing dependencies
* copying code
* starting application

Example:

```dockerfile
FROM node:20-alpine

WORKDIR /app

COPY . .

RUN npm install

CMD ["npm", "start"]
```

Dockerfile usually SHOULD NOT contain secrets.

---

# Docker Compose Responsibility

Docker Compose is used for:

* starting containers
* networking
* volumes
* environment variables
* container configuration

Example:

```yaml
environment:
  SECRET_NAME: prod/postgres
```

Compose acts like:

```text
Configuration Injector
```

---

# Backend Application Responsibility

Backend application:

* reads environment variables
* fetches AWS secrets
* parses JSON
* connects to DB
* uses API keys

Example:

```javascript
process.env.SECRET_NAME
```

---

# Real Responsibility Split

| Component      | Responsibility                 |
| -------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Dockerfile     | Build image                    |
| Docker Compose | Configure containers           |
| Backend App    | Use secrets and business logic |

---

# 12. IAM Role Flow

AWS Secrets Manager access requires permissions.

Production systems use:

```text
IAM Roles
```

instead of AWS access keys.

---

# Real Flow

```text
EC2 Instance
    ↓
IAM Role Attached
    ↓
Container Inherits Permissions
    ↓
Application Calls AWS Secrets Manager
```

---

# Example IAM Policy

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "secretsmanager:GetSecretValue"
      ],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
```

---

